WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.920
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.600
BRIAN PAGANO:
Project-thinking rewards

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.950
doing the minimum to meet
the project's specifications,

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:29.270
the project's scope, and not
screwing up while doing it.

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:31.020
And it's not very
customer-focused, right?

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.450
Your focus when you're
working on a project

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:38.910
is to succeed by the minimum
standards of the project,

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:43.630
and most often you're never
even talking to a customer.

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:47.140
But that's not even
the worst part.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:48.820
That's not even
the biggest issue

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:53.050
of why I get upset
when we talk about--

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:55.600
when I say stop
thinking about APIs

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:00.950
as projects is that projects are
planned work with an end date.

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:05.420
An end date-- like,
APIs drive mobile.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:06.950
They drive web.

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.947
They drive IOT, all the smart
cars, all the smart appliances,

00:01:09.947 --> 00:01:11.030
your smart vacuum cleaner.

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:13.770
All these things--
that's not going away.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:16.080
That's only getting bigger
and bigger and bigger,

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:19.280
a huge part of our
lives, the main core

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.810
part of how we do business.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:22.870
So why would that
have an end date?

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:26.130
That doesn't make
any sense at all.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.780
So when I come and visit you
folks at your various offices

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.030
and you tell me
that you're thinking

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:33.530
about becoming
API-centric, you're

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:35.570
thinking about
becoming a platform,

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.610
doing some digital
transformation,

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:39.640
you always ask, Brian,
what's the big mistake?

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:41.890
Like, you've seen people do
this all around the world.

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:45.256
What's the one big mistake
that we're likely to make?

00:01:45.256 --> 00:01:47.630
And the fact that you're tuned
in to a session like this,

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:49.338
the fact that you're
asking the question,

00:01:49.338 --> 00:01:51.860
means you might not
make that mistake.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:54.664
But the big mistake I see
is passing the potato.

00:01:54.664 --> 00:01:56.330
You're all wondering
why I'm showing you

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:58.820
a potato in the
middle of the workday,

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:01.640
it's because when you take the
hot potato and you pass it.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.810
So when I say to somebody,
let's talk about APIs,

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:06.450
they say, oh, let
me have you talk

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:09.250
to my director of operations.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:09.880
Wait a minute.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:12.430
I definitely should talk to
your director of operations

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:14.050
at some point,
just like I should

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.870
talk to your architects,
developers, security.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:18.700
But we're talking about
market share here.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.080
We're talking about revenue.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.330
We're talking about your brand.

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:25.090
We're talking about your partner
ecosystem, your developer

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:26.240
ecosystem.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:29.530
These are serious
high level topics.

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:33.310
These aren't
operational concerns.

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:34.690
And why does this happen?

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:37.090
It's because APIs sounds geeky.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:38.590
I wish there was a
way that we could

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:42.790
have these conversations without
even using the word "API."

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:43.460
Because it does.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:45.140
It sounds super
technical, super geeky,

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:48.510
and there's obviously a deep
technical component to it.

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:51.570
But APIs are so
much more than that.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:53.810
Now, OK, Brian, if
they're not a project--

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:57.350
if you're telling us stop
thinking of APIs as a project--

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:58.260
then what are they?

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.120
How should we think about APIs?

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.030
Well, let's think about
that for a second.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:06.630
APIs might be a program.

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:09.160
We all know what a program
is, a cluster of projects.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:10.110
So this is better.

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:12.720
If I come to visit your office
and you say, hey, Brian,

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.520
I want to talk to you
about our API program,

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:16.600
I'll be a little happier.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:18.600
Because that shows that
you're thinking about it

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.730
in broader terms
that you recognize

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:22.650
that this is technology
that you can leverage

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:25.020
across multiple
lines of business,

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.100
across multiple projects.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.140
That's great.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:32.280
Another possibility
is a platform.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.380
One slide that I
often like to show

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.950
is talking about the
most valuable companies

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:42.770
in the world today versus, say,
25 years ago, 15 years ago.

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:45.270
If we look at the most
valuable companies in the world

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:49.440
from 15 years ago, 25 years ago,
it's all the usual suspects.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.000
It's all the people
you'd expect--

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.850
your giant petroleum
companies, General Motors,

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:55.772
the big industrial companies.

00:03:55.772 --> 00:03:58.230
And if you look at the most
valuable companies in the world

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:04.810
from last year, it's
Apple and Google Alphabet

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:08.290
and it's Microsoft and
Facebook and Amazon.

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:10.090
And always people say,
oh, Brian, I get it.

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:12.100
It's Silicon Valley companies.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:13.240
Wrong.

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:15.512
I mean, yeah, they all are
Silicon Valley companies.

00:04:15.512 --> 00:04:17.470
But there are a lot of
Silicon Valley companies

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:19.149
that aren't on that list.

00:04:19.149 --> 00:04:19.990
OK, Brian, I get it.

00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:21.430
They're all software companies.

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:22.300
Wrong.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:24.215
I mean, yes, they are
all software companies.

00:04:24.215 --> 00:04:25.840
But there's a lot of
software companies

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:27.650
that aren't on that list.

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:30.075
If you look at the most
valuable companies in the world,

00:04:30.075 --> 00:04:31.450
the thing that
you'll immediately

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:34.470
notice that they
all have in common

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:37.330
is that they're all platforms.

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:40.920
That means they allow
other people outside

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:43.080
of traditional
organizational boundaries

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:46.246
to participate in their success.

00:04:46.246 --> 00:04:47.620
Now, this is very,
very powerful,

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:50.050
because this is no longer
a hypothesis or a guess.

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:52.330
This is now empirical
evidence of how

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:54.940
you succeed in the
21st century, which

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:57.720
is very different from how you
succeeded in the 20th century.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:59.320
We all learned
how to do business

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:03.786
by raising barriers to entry
instead of lowering barriers

00:05:03.786 --> 00:05:06.160
to cooperation, which is how
you win in the 21st century.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:07.780
That is a pretty big shift.

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:09.939
And again, while some of
this can seem semantic,

00:05:09.939 --> 00:05:11.980
think about the 1,000
decisions that your company

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:13.850
is going to make this month.

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:15.820
If you're making them
on the 20th century

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:17.890
model of raising
barrier to entry,

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:20.440
versus the 21st century
model of becoming

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:23.740
a platform, of lowering
barriers to cooperation,

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:27.130
that's going to result in
a very different outcome

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:28.840
for the success of
your company, possibly

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:31.290
the survival of your company.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:33.409
Now, APIs might be a program.

00:05:33.409 --> 00:05:34.450
That might be a platform.

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:35.775
I'd be quite
pleased if I came in

00:05:35.775 --> 00:05:38.150
and you wanted to talk to me
about your platform project,

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:40.420
turning your business
into a platform.

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:45.250
Because honestly, if you're not
creating a digital ecosystem,

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:48.190
or participating
in an ecosystem,

00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:51.040
you probably only
have a couple of years

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:53.890
to continue doing business
in your current form.

00:05:53.890 --> 00:05:56.320
Now, that can mean a
lot of different things.

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:58.090
And I don't mean to
sound too gloomy,

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:00.484
but this is the reality
of today's market.

00:06:00.484 --> 00:06:01.900
Now, the third
possibility of what

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:06.340
an API is if it's not a
project is a way of life.

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:09.100
APIs might just be
the kind of thing

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:12.010
that become ubiquitous in
our business and technology.

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:13.460
Now, why would I say that?

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:15.560
Obviously I love APIs.

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:19.420
I have the sticker on my
laptops and the sticker

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:20.200
on my briefcase.

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:23.080
And I dream about this
stuff when I'm at home.

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:25.750
But APIs power
customer experience.

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:28.810
And the second big mistake I see
most companies making today--

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:31.510
most of these big traditional
powerhouse companies when I

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:33.000
come to visit your office is--

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.650
is that it's so inwardly
focused and so focused

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:39.580
on your existing data centers,
your existing technologies.

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:42.280
And the battle right
now is on the front end.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:44.230
The battle is about
the experiences.

00:06:44.230 --> 00:06:47.007
Now, the people on the front
end may be your employees.

00:06:47.007 --> 00:06:48.340
It doesn't have to be customers.

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:50.390
It's often customers.

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:53.200
It can be a point of
sale people at stores.

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:56.280
It can be your partners and your
supply chain, your employees,

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:57.970
any one of these people.

00:06:57.970 --> 00:07:00.820
They are demanding a
better customer experience,

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:03.250
because we're all
doing business in 2018,

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:04.340
whether we want to or not.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:05.740
We can't say we're not ready.

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:09.550
And doing business in 2018
means that everybody expects

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:11.640
you to get to market faster.

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:13.450
That means that they
expect self-service.

00:07:13.450 --> 00:07:16.000
They expect you to be on the
platforms on which they're

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:17.650
happiest and most productive.

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:19.571
We've all been trained
to expect these things,

00:07:19.571 --> 00:07:21.070
and there's really
no way out of it.

00:07:21.070 --> 00:07:23.290
And yet, I see so many
companies still focusing

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:26.980
on eeking an extra 5% of
performance out of the backend

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:30.550
and not focused on delighting
their users with great customer

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:33.460
experiences, like APIs
would help them to do.

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:35.860
APIs also enable the
ecosystems and platforms.

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:38.020
I just talked about
how essentially,

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:42.130
empirically the way to
win in the 21st century

00:07:42.130 --> 00:07:45.610
is to become a platform or
to participate on a platform,

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:49.330
to become an ecosystem or to
participate in an ecosystem.

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:51.490
There really is no other answer.

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:55.810
And it just so happens that
APIs are a fantastic way

00:07:55.810 --> 00:07:59.170
to allow your company
to do these things.

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:03.645
Now, APIs drive
revenue and growth.

00:08:03.645 --> 00:08:04.270
Think about it.

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:06.540
If you could suddenly
get your apps

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:10.580
onto a bunch of new devices,
just statistically speaking,

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:12.940
you have a bigger
addressable market.

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:16.870
If you can make changes
faster, if you can say yes

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:19.600
to more partnerships-- that
old kind of Hollywood model

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:21.460
where they make 10
movies, and eight of them

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:24.380
lose money, and the ninth one
breaks even, and the 10th one

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:27.190
is a blockbuster and pays
for the next 10 films.

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:29.620
The problem is they don't
know which one that is,

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:32.020
so they have to make
the next 10 films.

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:35.020
Partnerships and technology
experiments in the company

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:37.539
can feel that way, that
most of them lose money.

00:08:37.539 --> 00:08:39.400
Most them aren't worth
the time and effort.

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:42.352
What if there was a
self-service platform?

00:08:42.352 --> 00:08:44.560
What if there was a technology
and a methodology that

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:47.950
would help us to just say
yes to every partner who

00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:51.340
comes and says, we'd like to do
some business together and see

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:53.330
which one is the blockbuster.

00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:56.260
And if you say yes to every
10, statistically over time,

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:58.420
you're going to discover
new revenue streams.

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:01.030
You're going to discover
that your business has

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:03.850
a bunch of exciting new
partnerships and products that

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:05.950
really would never
have come organically

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:08.440
from inside the organization.

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:11.500
Now, whenever we have
new folks starting

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:15.034
at Apigee, and especially
like a new PR firm or somebody

00:09:15.034 --> 00:09:16.450
wants to understand,
Brian, what's

00:09:16.450 --> 00:09:19.620
the reason why so
many of our customers

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:21.400
buy the Apigee product?

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:22.450
And I say, there's a lot.

00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:25.840
There's security, and scale,
and ease of use, and experience.

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:27.740
I mean, there's a
lot of good reasons.

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:33.180
But I think fundamentally,
it comes down to speed.

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:37.050
Every CIO I talk to, every CTO
I talk to around the world,

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:40.650
without fail, says,
the business is crazy.

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:43.320
They're asking me to move faster
than we've ever moved before,

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:46.050
to release on multiple
platforms simultaneously.

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:49.600
We're just, frankly,
not set up for that.

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:54.500
And every time I talk to the
CMO, they say my IT is broken.

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:55.270
They're too slow.

00:09:55.270 --> 00:09:56.186
They're too expensive.

00:09:56.186 --> 00:09:58.940
And they keep saying
no to everything.

00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:02.470
So what if there were a
technology and a methodology

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:07.970
like APIs that would allow you
to decouple those two worlds?

00:10:07.970 --> 00:10:09.860
That's what APIs
do, fundamentally,

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:12.410
is to help you move
faster to allow each side

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:13.590
to move at its own speed.

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:17.420
You've heard two
speed IT, and that's

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:19.640
a somewhat judgment-laden term.

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:21.680
But you get the
idea, that each end,

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:24.470
the front end and the back end,
can move at their own pace.

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:27.560
APIs are a perfect
tool for enabling that.

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:29.780
So, OK, that's starting
not to sound so geeky.

00:10:29.780 --> 00:10:32.750
We talked about why this often
that hot potato gets passed,

00:10:32.750 --> 00:10:34.790
because this sounds like
a really geeky topic.

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:37.010
But if we're talking
about customer experience,

00:10:37.010 --> 00:10:38.900
our customer
satisfaction, they're

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:41.210
not eroding our market
share, holding off

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:43.640
some of our disruptors or
traditional competitors,

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:47.570
if we're talking about
participating in an ecosystem,

00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:49.640
our business itself
becoming a platform

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:51.904
and becoming one of those
winners in the 21st century

00:10:51.904 --> 00:10:54.320
like we looked at before, if
we're talking about something

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:57.020
that may contribute
to revenue and growth

00:10:57.020 --> 00:10:59.540
and something that's going
to help us move faster

00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:01.100
so that our customers
are happier,

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:04.790
our employees are happier, and
as IT, the business gets off

00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:07.670
our backs and says, OK,
you guys are delivering

00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:09.534
exactly what we
need you to deliver,

00:11:09.534 --> 00:11:10.700
that doesn't sound so geeky.

00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:12.780
That sounds really important.

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:14.660
And hopefully you'll
see that those

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:17.420
are the kind of conversations
we should be having when we come

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:19.000
and visit.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:23.440
Now, philosophy, logic,
a bit of a hobby of mine.

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:25.700
So I like the idea of
a category mistake.

00:11:25.700 --> 00:11:27.700
And for those who don't
know, a category mistake

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:32.110
was an idea created by the 20th
century philosopher Gilbert

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:33.340
Ryle.

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:34.810
He was at Cambridge or Oxford.

00:11:34.810 --> 00:11:35.920
I can never keep
those two straight.

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:37.120
Let's say it was Cambridge.

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:40.000
And he took his friend
to visit the university

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:43.630
and showed them the dining
hall and the dormitories

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:45.250
and the lecture
halls and all that.

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:46.960
And the friend said,
this is all great.

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:48.860
Where's Cambridge?

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:50.610
So the friend had made
a category mistake.

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:52.970
They thought Cambridge
was a type of building,

00:11:52.970 --> 00:11:56.200
and Cambridge is a
type of university.

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:59.730
It's a very powerful
tool to understand where

00:11:59.730 --> 00:12:01.041
companies are making mistakes.

00:12:01.041 --> 00:12:02.790
So companies are making
a category mistake

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:05.820
by thinking that some
business-level catalyst,

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:08.940
something that affects customer
satisfaction, that affects

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:12.390
revenue, that affects
partnerships, that affects time

00:12:12.390 --> 00:12:16.849
to market, is somehow
an operational issue.

00:12:16.849 --> 00:12:19.140
This is so mind blowing to
me that I often think of it.

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:21.750
It's like saying that,
well, our company's revenue,

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:23.910
it's really mostly a
pencil-sharpening issue.

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:25.620
I mean, it's the
operational aspect there

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:26.910
of sharpening that pencil.

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:27.930
Of course not.

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:29.490
Sharpening that
pencil has nothing

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:30.990
to do with your
company's revenue.

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:33.780
That is a complete
category mistake.

00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:35.590
But how does this happen?

00:12:35.590 --> 00:12:38.670
So, when I was a technology
executive in New York,

00:12:38.670 --> 00:12:40.050
my teams would often come to me.

00:12:40.050 --> 00:12:41.758
They'd come into my
office in the morning

00:12:41.758 --> 00:12:44.480
and say, Brian, that
guy is being a jerk.

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:46.699
And I'd say, I don't think so.

00:12:46.699 --> 00:12:47.490
I don't believe it.

00:12:47.490 --> 00:12:48.930
I mean, it was Wall Street.

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:49.950
It's possible.

00:12:49.950 --> 00:12:52.740
But I don't believe anybody
woke up this morning,

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:56.312
looked in the mirror and said,
I'm going to be a jerk today.

00:12:56.312 --> 00:12:58.020
So we need to find
some other explanation

00:12:58.020 --> 00:12:59.490
for what's going on.

00:12:59.490 --> 00:13:03.931
This person thinks that their
mandate is such and such.

00:13:03.931 --> 00:13:06.180
And they think they're
executing against that mandate.

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:09.226
So maybe they don't understand
the scope of their job.

00:13:09.226 --> 00:13:11.850
Maybe they don't understand the
priorities of the organization.

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:15.630
There's some other motivation,
I think, than just being a jerk.

00:13:15.630 --> 00:13:18.150
Now, we know that some
of the miscommunication

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:22.000
happens because
APIs sound geeky.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:24.690
But every organization
has blind spots.

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:27.100
And just like I was talking
about the folks coming

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:29.920
into my office in New
York, I notice there's

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:31.810
a lot of miscommunication.

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:35.050
On the one side, we have
business which is obviously

00:13:35.050 --> 00:13:36.760
trying to do the
right thing, keep

00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:39.866
everybody gainfully employed,
delight the customers.

00:13:39.866 --> 00:13:41.740
And on the other side,
you've got technology,

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:43.698
which is trying to do
what it's supposed to do,

00:13:43.698 --> 00:13:46.330
which is prevent breaches, make
sure that disaster recovery

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:48.100
and business
continuity are covered,

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:50.580
audits, all these great things.

00:13:50.580 --> 00:13:54.220
So you have two smart groups of
people who are both competent,

00:13:54.220 --> 00:13:57.110
trying to do the right
thing, and yet, we

00:13:57.110 --> 00:13:59.000
end up in the situation
we're in today,

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:01.790
which is, it takes us too long
to get new things to market.

00:14:01.790 --> 00:14:03.160
Our customers aren't happy.

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:04.630
We're not on all the devices.

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:07.720
The apps aren't as good as
some of the startup, you know,

00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:09.160
flashy apps.

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:12.080
How can we not get this solved?

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:14.420
And that's because there's
this chasm where there's

00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:18.500
a language and a process
barrier between these two worlds

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:21.290
and everybody thinks they're
saying the same thing.

00:14:21.290 --> 00:14:25.190
And yet, it's not
resulting in the positive.

00:14:25.190 --> 00:14:28.760
So I was told a few years
ago a story of the CEO of one

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:31.469
of the major world airlines who
was flying for whatever reason

00:14:31.469 --> 00:14:32.510
on a competitor's flight.

00:14:32.510 --> 00:14:35.465
They didn't have a route
that flew that way.

00:14:35.465 --> 00:14:36.590
This was several years ago.

00:14:36.590 --> 00:14:40.040
He noticed that they had
media controls in the armrest.

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:42.120
And he said, this is fantastic.

00:14:42.120 --> 00:14:44.000
We have to do this
for our customers.

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:46.520
And he landed, and he
called up his lieutenants.

00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:48.920
And he said, you
know, we have to make

00:14:48.920 --> 00:14:50.430
these things for our customers.

00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:52.440
And so this trickled
through the organization.

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:54.500
Let's put media
controls on the armrest

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:55.700
to delight our customers.

00:14:55.700 --> 00:14:57.080
And what do you
suppose happened?

00:14:57.080 --> 00:14:58.820
Each group started
saying, well, we

00:14:58.820 --> 00:15:00.170
can use the existing arm rest.

00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:02.510
We'll just drill a hole
underneath and kind

00:15:02.510 --> 00:15:04.304
of superglue a little
panel in there.

00:15:04.304 --> 00:15:06.470
And you guys all remember
this from a few years ago.

00:15:06.470 --> 00:15:08.220
Where you'd fall
asleep on an airplane,

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:09.428
and it's like a gravity well.

00:15:09.428 --> 00:15:10.760
Your arm would get sucked in.

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:13.724
It would start to fall asleep
and tingle in really bad ways.

00:15:13.724 --> 00:15:15.640
Your elbow would always
hit the volume button,

00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:16.460
things like that.

00:15:16.460 --> 00:15:17.582
It was miserable.

00:15:17.582 --> 00:15:19.790
And if you think about a
plane with 300 people on it,

00:15:19.790 --> 00:15:24.260
you're talking months of
person hours of misery

00:15:24.260 --> 00:15:26.300
being added to every flight.

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:31.240
And the goal was, you recall,
delight our customers.

00:15:31.240 --> 00:15:33.079
But it got mistranslated
as it passed

00:15:33.079 --> 00:15:34.120
through the organization.

00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:36.580
And some people heard
that as, save money.

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:39.890
Some people thought that meant,
let's be as clever as we can,

00:15:39.890 --> 00:15:41.380
reusing existing parts.

00:15:41.380 --> 00:15:43.425
It got all good
people all trying

00:15:43.425 --> 00:15:44.800
to do something
that they thought

00:15:44.800 --> 00:15:45.940
was the right thing to do.

00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:48.087
And yet it resulted in misery.

00:15:48.087 --> 00:15:50.170
Fortunately, we have gone
to the touch screen era.

00:15:50.170 --> 00:15:51.800
We're not really
doing that anymore.

00:15:51.800 --> 00:15:54.610
As a very frequent flyer,
this is great for me.

00:15:54.610 --> 00:15:57.130
But this to me
starts to explain how

00:15:57.130 --> 00:15:59.800
we have two different
worlds and how we can end up

00:15:59.800 --> 00:16:01.150
in this crazy situation.

00:16:01.150 --> 00:16:02.590
Now, I know it
can sound like I'm

00:16:02.590 --> 00:16:04.240
saying a lot of doom and gloom.

00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:06.080
But let's talk about
some good news.

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:08.730
There is a way to solve this.

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:10.480
I know there's a lot
to look at when we're

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:13.650
looking at all the other things
other than simply deploying

00:16:13.650 --> 00:16:17.100
a technology, configuring a
technology, when we're talking

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:19.600
about governance and funding
and all these various bits that

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:21.725
go around it that just
kind of have to be done

00:16:21.725 --> 00:16:23.600
and are very important
to getting things done

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:26.830
but they're not the
cool technology bits.

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:28.926
If you look at the
problem domain--

00:16:28.926 --> 00:16:30.550
so this is the third
big mistake that I

00:16:30.550 --> 00:16:32.530
see companies making,
is they try to solve

00:16:32.530 --> 00:16:34.610
one sixth of the problem.

00:16:34.610 --> 00:16:35.982
And I don't get that at all.

00:16:35.982 --> 00:16:38.440
You guys have a bunch of stuff
that you've built and bought

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:39.700
over the years.

00:16:39.700 --> 00:16:42.700
You have an end user-- often
a customer maybe an employee--

00:16:42.700 --> 00:16:43.690
on the other side.

00:16:43.690 --> 00:16:47.260
If you can't get from
point A to point B,

00:16:47.260 --> 00:16:48.727
you have not solved the problem.

00:16:48.727 --> 00:16:51.310
And so my mind is blown all the
time when people come and talk

00:16:51.310 --> 00:16:51.810
to me.

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:54.690
Well, we found a vendor that
will sell us a developer web

00:16:54.690 --> 00:16:56.000
site.

00:16:56.000 --> 00:16:56.600
Great.

00:16:56.600 --> 00:16:57.808
You need a developer website.

00:16:57.808 --> 00:16:58.990
It's necessary.

00:16:58.990 --> 00:17:00.560
It's not sufficient.

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:03.048
I mean, what happens when
they start building apps?

00:17:03.048 --> 00:17:05.089
What happens when traffic
starts flowing through?

00:17:05.089 --> 00:17:07.214
Don't you need scale and
security and monetization,

00:17:07.214 --> 00:17:08.359
all these other things?

00:17:08.359 --> 00:17:11.210
Or my ESB vendor or
somebody on the back end

00:17:11.210 --> 00:17:12.890
has allowed me to
do some exposure.

00:17:12.890 --> 00:17:13.440
Great.

00:17:13.440 --> 00:17:14.910
Exposure is not the problem.

00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:18.240
And a runtime is critical.

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:20.432
It's necessary, but
it's not sufficient.

00:17:20.432 --> 00:17:21.890
What about all the
developer tools?

00:17:21.890 --> 00:17:22.700
What about analytics?

00:17:22.700 --> 00:17:23.950
What about all the other pieces?

00:17:23.950 --> 00:17:25.574
You've got to get
from point A to point

00:17:25.574 --> 00:17:26.915
B. That's the problem domain.

00:17:26.915 --> 00:17:28.040
And of course, the players.

00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:29.330
Who's involved in this?

00:17:29.330 --> 00:17:32.320
Now, the problem domain
can be easily visualized,

00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:34.070
as I said, from all
the stuff you've built

00:17:34.070 --> 00:17:35.870
and bought all the way
out to the end user.

00:17:35.870 --> 00:17:38.210
It says customer here,
but just any end user.

00:17:38.210 --> 00:17:40.251
And I think you'd agree
with me that if you don't

00:17:40.251 --> 00:17:42.230
get from point A to
point B, you simply

00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:43.467
haven't solved the problem.

00:17:43.467 --> 00:17:45.050
And that's why it's
so confusing to me

00:17:45.050 --> 00:17:46.550
when people talk
to me about solving

00:17:46.550 --> 00:17:48.330
one sixth of the problem.

00:17:48.330 --> 00:17:49.940
It makes no sense.

00:17:49.940 --> 00:17:52.970
Now, we know the players that
are involved in this too.

00:17:52.970 --> 00:17:55.640
So I said, if you can identify
the entire problem domain

00:17:55.640 --> 00:17:57.680
and identify who
the players are,

00:17:57.680 --> 00:17:59.154
it starts to make more sense.

00:17:59.154 --> 00:18:00.570
Because some people
think, oh, no.

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:01.580
They're cookie lickers.

00:18:01.580 --> 00:18:03.170
You know that idea,
like, when you were 10

00:18:03.170 --> 00:18:04.120
and you didn't
want your siblings

00:18:04.120 --> 00:18:05.960
to get one of the cookies,
so you lick all the cookies

00:18:05.960 --> 00:18:07.190
so nobody else can eat one.

00:18:07.190 --> 00:18:09.500
This happens in organizations
all the time too.

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:11.900
I'm going to lick this
cookie, and this is my cookie

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:13.190
and no one else gets the play.

00:18:13.190 --> 00:18:15.470
So it's good to get
on the white board,

00:18:15.470 --> 00:18:19.220
on the screen, whatever,
who is involved here,

00:18:19.220 --> 00:18:20.720
who are the other players.

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:23.520
Another way to look at it, a
more abstract way is to say,

00:18:23.520 --> 00:18:26.600
OK, we've got lines of
business and generally

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:29.090
in a federated
technology organization,

00:18:29.090 --> 00:18:30.980
they've got a line
of business tech.

00:18:30.980 --> 00:18:32.840
But we've also got
the brand, and we've

00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:34.430
got enterprise technology.

00:18:34.430 --> 00:18:37.100
And there's usually a kind
of an unhealthy tension

00:18:37.100 --> 00:18:39.230
between these two things
when fundamentally they

00:18:39.230 --> 00:18:40.580
all want the same thing.

00:18:40.580 --> 00:18:41.960
And I've been very
pleased to see

00:18:41.960 --> 00:18:45.860
how much APIs tend to reduce
this tension between these two

00:18:45.860 --> 00:18:47.520
groups.

00:18:47.520 --> 00:18:51.290
So since APIs are just going to
be part of how we do business

00:18:51.290 --> 00:18:53.480
and live our lives from
now on, like water to fish,

00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:56.430
it's just going to be
ubiquitous in how we live.

00:18:56.430 --> 00:18:58.490
Think about how to
streamline governance.

00:18:58.490 --> 00:19:02.360
So I know some people are
very extreme in their view

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:03.917
that APIs make
governance go away.

00:19:03.917 --> 00:19:05.750
I don't believe that
coming from Big Fortune

00:19:05.750 --> 00:19:07.970
500 companies myself.

00:19:07.970 --> 00:19:10.050
I think that it does
streamline governance.

00:19:10.050 --> 00:19:13.040
I think a RESTful API,
just by necessity,

00:19:13.040 --> 00:19:16.600
needs less governance
than an old fashioned soap

00:19:16.600 --> 00:19:18.560
contract would, for example.

00:19:18.560 --> 00:19:20.240
To think about, it's
the classic debate

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:21.621
of who funds these things.

00:19:21.621 --> 00:19:23.120
The groups that are
going to benefit

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:27.500
most from their consumption, or
the backend teams themselves?

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:28.875
I mean, think
about realistically

00:19:28.875 --> 00:19:30.041
how we can fund this better.

00:19:30.041 --> 00:19:32.420
Because I talk to so many
organizations when we come in

00:19:32.420 --> 00:19:33.450
and help them.

00:19:33.450 --> 00:19:37.520
And it turns out that they feel
like they can not do business

00:19:37.520 --> 00:19:40.550
as a brand or across
whatever organizational trust

00:19:40.550 --> 00:19:42.820
boundary, whether that's
a department, division,

00:19:42.820 --> 00:19:44.200
line of business, whatever.

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:46.770
But APIs give you
this abstraction layer

00:19:46.770 --> 00:19:49.190
and say, I don't really care
how they implemented this.

00:19:49.190 --> 00:19:50.960
I don't really care
what a customer

00:19:50.960 --> 00:19:52.310
looks like in their database.

00:19:52.310 --> 00:19:54.840
I don't even truly need
a single source of truth.

00:19:54.840 --> 00:19:57.410
We've now got an interface
that all of our developers

00:19:57.410 --> 00:20:00.600
know how to use, all of our
architects know how to use.

00:20:00.600 --> 00:20:02.750
And now we can go forward
and do clever things

00:20:02.750 --> 00:20:05.300
like the commercial models
to say, how could we

00:20:05.300 --> 00:20:06.590
charge differently now?

00:20:06.590 --> 00:20:08.780
Could we now have
subscription offerings?

00:20:08.780 --> 00:20:10.010
Could we do revenue sharing?

00:20:10.010 --> 00:20:11.510
Could we do advertising
based stuff?

00:20:11.510 --> 00:20:13.460
There's a lot of
different ways to make

00:20:13.460 --> 00:20:16.130
money that might not have
been available to you

00:20:16.130 --> 00:20:18.300
before you became API centric.

00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:20.330
So APIs, in my
experience, tend to be

00:20:20.330 --> 00:20:23.930
a forcing function for an
organization and the secret--

00:20:23.930 --> 00:20:26.180
that's why it's not in tiny
print, it's in big print--

00:20:26.180 --> 00:20:27.930
is that the challenge
won't be technology.

00:20:27.930 --> 00:20:30.990
This technology-- just the
Apigee platform itself,

00:20:30.990 --> 00:20:32.700
for example, as one example--

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:34.610
it just has billions
and billions

00:20:34.610 --> 00:20:36.060
of calls going through it.

00:20:36.060 --> 00:20:38.420
It's fourth
generation technology.

00:20:38.420 --> 00:20:41.360
The technology is
well and truly solved.

00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:43.170
It's the other bits
of the organization.

00:20:43.170 --> 00:20:45.980
Can we switch from being
a 20th century company

00:20:45.980 --> 00:20:48.620
to a 21st century mindset?

00:20:48.620 --> 00:20:51.380
Can we start thinking
in terms of not

00:20:51.380 --> 00:20:54.650
being reduced to
blindly following

00:20:54.650 --> 00:20:57.140
our governance and our
existing processes but think

00:20:57.140 --> 00:20:59.870
about customer delight, think
about that problem domain

00:20:59.870 --> 00:21:02.599
and how we can work
together to solve that?

00:21:02.599 --> 00:21:04.140
So how do we start
doing that, Brian?

00:21:04.140 --> 00:21:04.650
That all sounds great.

00:21:04.650 --> 00:21:06.233
That's like a
performance report where

00:21:06.233 --> 00:21:07.905
your boss says, do better.

00:21:07.905 --> 00:21:09.030
That's not very actionable.

00:21:09.030 --> 00:21:10.300
You can't do much with it.

00:21:10.300 --> 00:21:13.660
So I would say, hey, you
can start with a single API.

00:21:13.660 --> 00:21:14.910
You've got to start somewhere.

00:21:14.910 --> 00:21:15.951
Try to make it a key API.

00:21:15.951 --> 00:21:18.210
When I work with retail
companies and say,

00:21:18.210 --> 00:21:19.710
we'll start with
a store locator,

00:21:19.710 --> 00:21:21.670
I think, you know,
no one's going

00:21:21.670 --> 00:21:24.240
to get fired if the
store locator fails.

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:25.380
That's not critical path.

00:21:25.380 --> 00:21:27.970
You need to do something that's
going to be critical path.

00:21:27.970 --> 00:21:29.971
Recognize that an operational
aspect will exist.

00:21:29.971 --> 00:21:31.928
Whoa, whoa, whoa, Brian,
you've been telling us

00:21:31.928 --> 00:21:32.670
the whole time--

00:21:32.670 --> 00:21:33.810
this isn't an operational issue.

00:21:33.810 --> 00:21:34.893
That's a category mistake.

00:21:34.893 --> 00:21:35.880
Right.

00:21:35.880 --> 00:21:37.470
It is a category
mistake, but that

00:21:37.470 --> 00:21:40.080
doesn't mean there's not an
operational aspect to it.

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:42.090
And it would be just
as foolish to ignore

00:21:42.090 --> 00:21:46.161
the security, the
day-to-day operations,

00:21:46.161 --> 00:21:47.160
and try some new things.

00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:49.170
People ask me all
over the world,

00:21:49.170 --> 00:21:51.107
what makes Silicon
Valley so different?

00:21:51.107 --> 00:21:52.190
You've worked in New York.

00:21:52.190 --> 00:21:53.190
You've worked in Europe.

00:21:53.190 --> 00:21:55.600
You know, what makes
things so different here?

00:21:55.600 --> 00:21:57.320
And I think one of
the big key factors--

00:21:57.320 --> 00:22:00.270
it's just my opinion--
is that in Silicon Valley

00:22:00.270 --> 00:22:01.480
it's OK to fail.

00:22:01.480 --> 00:22:04.170
In fact, failing
fast is a good idea

00:22:04.170 --> 00:22:06.762
and gives you data that you
wouldn't get any other way.

00:22:06.762 --> 00:22:08.470
It gives you a tremendous
amount of data.

00:22:08.470 --> 00:22:09.970
Now, when I was in
New York, I often

00:22:09.970 --> 00:22:15.850
felt like if I had a big
marquee project that failed,

00:22:15.850 --> 00:22:18.755
it might torpedo my
career at that company.

00:22:18.755 --> 00:22:20.880
That might be enough to
stop my forward advancement

00:22:20.880 --> 00:22:21.840
at that company.

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:23.760
And that's simply not
the case out here.

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:26.190
And I encourage people to
try to adopt that mindset,

00:22:26.190 --> 00:22:28.560
that nobody gets an API
right on the first try.

00:22:28.560 --> 00:22:29.060
Nobody.

00:22:29.060 --> 00:22:31.185
You're usually going to
rev it at least three times

00:22:31.185 --> 00:22:32.010
the first year.

00:22:32.010 --> 00:22:33.960
So be OK with that.

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:36.300
APIs, they're a facade.

00:22:36.300 --> 00:22:39.060
So they're not
heavy and expensive

00:22:39.060 --> 00:22:40.860
like a backend system.

00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:43.230
And people seem to have
trouble making this mind shift.

00:22:43.230 --> 00:22:46.530
No, it's OK to experiment
and prototype with APIs.

00:22:46.530 --> 00:22:50.010
So one of the things we do to
understand this market better

00:22:50.010 --> 00:22:51.510
and to help our
customers understand

00:22:51.510 --> 00:22:54.240
themselves better is we have
this tool called Compass.

00:22:54.240 --> 00:22:56.352
You can find it at
compass.apigee.com.

00:22:56.352 --> 00:22:57.810
And it's a series
of questions that

00:22:57.810 --> 00:23:00.450
help basically to assess
the digital maturity

00:23:00.450 --> 00:23:01.690
of an organization.

00:23:01.690 --> 00:23:03.990
Now, most of the folks that
will be listening to this,

00:23:03.990 --> 00:23:05.615
you are the ones
who already get it.

00:23:05.615 --> 00:23:06.990
You're the ones
who already know.

00:23:06.990 --> 00:23:08.698
But you probably have
a lot of colleagues

00:23:08.698 --> 00:23:10.767
around your organization
who don't get it,

00:23:10.767 --> 00:23:12.600
who wonder why it's not
still OK-- you know,

00:23:12.600 --> 00:23:14.650
we did business like
this in 20th century.

00:23:14.650 --> 00:23:16.170
And it made us very successful.

00:23:16.170 --> 00:23:18.000
And you know, why
would we change that?

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:18.940
Or, you know what?

00:23:18.940 --> 00:23:19.731
We don't need help.

00:23:19.731 --> 00:23:21.136
We are already digital.

00:23:21.136 --> 00:23:22.260
We're already transforming.

00:23:22.260 --> 00:23:23.676
We are already
doing these things.

00:23:23.676 --> 00:23:27.259
And so what we found
is it's actually likely

00:23:27.259 --> 00:23:28.800
that some people in
your organization

00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:31.440
are saying the right
words, that somebody

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:34.514
is producing some kind of vision
plan, some kind of roadmap,

00:23:34.514 --> 00:23:36.180
that has some of the
right things on it.

00:23:36.180 --> 00:23:37.096
And that's great news.

00:23:37.096 --> 00:23:39.296
Because that wasn't the
case a couple of years ago.

00:23:39.296 --> 00:23:41.670
But we're really finding is
where companies are stumbling

00:23:41.670 --> 00:23:43.410
is around execution.

00:23:43.410 --> 00:23:45.900
And while it's great
to say the right words,

00:23:45.900 --> 00:23:50.310
it's got to have output
or it means nothing.

00:23:50.310 --> 00:23:52.052
And so we're finding
that companies just

00:23:52.052 --> 00:23:54.010
don't have the right
executive support-- again,

00:23:54.010 --> 00:23:57.810
that APIs sound very geeky.

00:23:57.810 --> 00:24:01.280
And they are not looking at
it across the organization

00:24:01.280 --> 00:24:03.210
and at the brand level
enough, and they're not

00:24:03.210 --> 00:24:05.070
thinking about
who's going to make

00:24:05.070 --> 00:24:08.260
it, who's going to support it,
and who's going to consume it.

00:24:08.260 --> 00:24:10.617
And therefore, that
nifty vision plan

00:24:10.617 --> 00:24:13.200
and all those buzzwords that are
being put on the white boards

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:14.390
really amount to nothing.

00:24:14.390 --> 00:24:16.140
It's even worse than
nothing, because it's

00:24:16.140 --> 00:24:18.540
fooling the organization
into thinking

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:19.865
that they're transforming.

00:24:19.865 --> 00:24:21.990
Now, another question I
get all the time is, Brian,

00:24:21.990 --> 00:24:25.830
we've invested in ESP
technologies, SOA technologies.

00:24:25.830 --> 00:24:27.390
So do we need this stuff?

00:24:27.390 --> 00:24:28.470
I think we're OK.

00:24:28.470 --> 00:24:30.545
And, you know, of
course you have.

00:24:30.545 --> 00:24:31.920
I mean, that's
what everybody did

00:24:31.920 --> 00:24:33.700
for the last 10 to 15 years.

00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:36.740
But those are
integration technologies.

00:24:36.740 --> 00:24:39.420
And the fact that your
integration technology

00:24:39.420 --> 00:24:41.940
will allow you to do
some exposure actually

00:24:41.940 --> 00:24:43.020
means nothing.

00:24:43.020 --> 00:24:45.090
Exposure is not the problem.

00:24:45.090 --> 00:24:46.590
Exposure is easy.

00:24:46.590 --> 00:24:48.870
Taking a complicated
thing and moving it

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:54.280
one layer forward in the
architecture solves nothing.

00:24:54.280 --> 00:24:56.410
If it's not being
consumed now, it's

00:24:56.410 --> 00:24:58.330
not going to be consumed then.

00:24:58.330 --> 00:24:59.810
This is all about consumption.

00:24:59.810 --> 00:25:02.600
This is a new layer
in the architecture.

00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:06.990
It generally sits in front of
the integration tier, the SOA

00:25:06.990 --> 00:25:08.050
and ESB kind of tier.

00:25:08.050 --> 00:25:10.450
This is the exposure
and consumption

00:25:10.450 --> 00:25:13.264
layer with a heavy
focus on consumption.

00:25:13.264 --> 00:25:14.680
Because when you
have transactions

00:25:14.680 --> 00:25:16.140
flowing through
the system, that's

00:25:16.140 --> 00:25:17.390
where your revenue comes from.

00:25:17.390 --> 00:25:19.270
That's where delighted
customers come from.

00:25:19.270 --> 00:25:22.630
When you have an API that
can be easily consumed,

00:25:22.630 --> 00:25:25.750
treated as a product
not a project--

00:25:25.750 --> 00:25:29.230
and think about all
the design implications

00:25:29.230 --> 00:25:31.260
of a product versus a project.

00:25:31.260 --> 00:25:33.010
We used to take photos
of doors every time

00:25:33.010 --> 00:25:36.430
we visited a company around
the world, not the Jim Morrison

00:25:36.430 --> 00:25:38.710
group, the actual doors on
the front of a building.

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:40.420
And what we noticed
is a lot of times

00:25:40.420 --> 00:25:43.180
there would be like a pull bar
and a little sign that said

00:25:43.180 --> 00:25:44.579
"push."

00:25:44.579 --> 00:25:46.120
Now, if you know
how our brains work,

00:25:46.120 --> 00:25:47.380
we have the lizard
brain that evolved

00:25:47.380 --> 00:25:48.460
over millions and
millions of years,

00:25:48.460 --> 00:25:50.980
and we have the frontal cortex,
which does verbal and is

00:25:50.980 --> 00:25:52.000
very, very new.

00:25:52.000 --> 00:25:53.530
And you know which
one wins when you

00:25:53.530 --> 00:25:56.380
have bad design and a
little bit of documentation?

00:25:56.380 --> 00:25:59.600
The lizard brain, every time,
right every single time.

00:25:59.600 --> 00:26:04.660
And so you can't paper over
bad design with documentation.

00:26:04.660 --> 00:26:06.370
That's what it means
to be a product,

00:26:06.370 --> 00:26:07.930
is to think about
the affordance,

00:26:07.930 --> 00:26:10.240
think about just by
looking at this API,

00:26:10.240 --> 00:26:12.700
I understand how they
want me to use it.

00:26:12.700 --> 00:26:16.170
Now, another mistake that I
see companies making is they

00:26:16.170 --> 00:26:17.745
don't respect the
developers enough.

00:26:17.745 --> 00:26:19.120
As much as I've
talked about this

00:26:19.120 --> 00:26:22.540
as being a business level, high
level technology leadership

00:26:22.540 --> 00:26:25.060
kind of issue, developers
are the kingmakers.

00:26:25.060 --> 00:26:27.880
APIs are essentially invisible.

00:26:27.880 --> 00:26:31.870
And if you don't have your
people, your contractors,

00:26:31.870 --> 00:26:34.570
your digital agencies,
your third parties building

00:26:34.570 --> 00:26:38.720
fantastic apps off of these
APIs, what's the point?

00:26:38.720 --> 00:26:40.552
They're essentially invisible.

00:26:40.552 --> 00:26:42.010
And then another
thing that I think

00:26:42.010 --> 00:26:43.930
is difficult for
traditional companies

00:26:43.930 --> 00:26:46.330
and traditional organizations
to get their heads around

00:26:46.330 --> 00:26:49.400
is that minimum viable
is still viable.

00:26:49.400 --> 00:26:53.270
So it's this idea, well, if we
don't have an 18-month project,

00:26:53.270 --> 00:26:55.670
if we don't have these golden
magical requirements that

00:26:55.670 --> 00:26:57.480
are somehow perfect
and will never change

00:26:57.480 --> 00:26:59.790
and I believe don't
exist, how are we

00:26:59.790 --> 00:27:01.280
ever going to produce anything?

00:27:01.280 --> 00:27:02.990
But the idea is, if
we can get something

00:27:02.990 --> 00:27:04.820
into the hands of
our users, especially

00:27:04.820 --> 00:27:06.350
if it's an internal project--

00:27:06.350 --> 00:27:09.290
so it's for some line of
business, some department--

00:27:09.290 --> 00:27:11.960
the faster we get
things into their hands,

00:27:11.960 --> 00:27:13.970
the faster we get
feedback about how

00:27:13.970 --> 00:27:16.290
this is used in the real world.

00:27:16.290 --> 00:27:19.400
And they say that no combat
plan survives contact

00:27:19.400 --> 00:27:22.130
with the enemy, and I've found
the same thing about APIs

00:27:22.130 --> 00:27:25.260
is that no API, no matter how
good your practitioners are,

00:27:25.260 --> 00:27:27.060
no matter how much
you know about APIs

00:27:27.060 --> 00:27:31.340
is no API survives first
contact with the developers.

00:27:31.340 --> 00:27:33.710
You need to see how the
API behaves in the wild,

00:27:33.710 --> 00:27:37.730
is consumed in the wild, to
really understand that API.

00:27:37.730 --> 00:27:41.570
And as I mentioned earlier,
if it came down to one thing,

00:27:41.570 --> 00:27:44.510
if I'm trying to convince my
colleagues about why we need

00:27:44.510 --> 00:27:47.900
to be API first, why we
need to be API centric,

00:27:47.900 --> 00:27:49.160
it's about speed.

00:27:49.160 --> 00:27:52.730
It simply allows our different
departments and groups

00:27:52.730 --> 00:27:53.810
to move faster.

00:27:53.810 --> 00:27:56.470
It allows us to get ideas
into production faster.

00:27:56.470 --> 00:27:58.790
It allows us to onboard
developers faster,

00:27:58.790 --> 00:28:03.800
to onboard partners faster,
to onboard digital agencies

00:28:03.800 --> 00:28:04.490
faster.

00:28:04.490 --> 00:28:08.310
It allows us to look more
modern and more disruptive,

00:28:08.310 --> 00:28:10.130
even if we consider
ourselves to be

00:28:10.130 --> 00:28:13.220
a big, traditional
bureaucratic organization.

00:28:13.220 --> 00:28:15.130
And then user
experience-- like I said,

00:28:15.130 --> 00:28:17.720
it's not the muscle memory
of most traditional companies

00:28:17.720 --> 00:28:19.350
to think in terms
of user experience.

00:28:19.350 --> 00:28:21.560
We've tended to have kind
of a captive audience.

00:28:21.560 --> 00:28:23.060
It's OK if we're a
generation or two

00:28:23.060 --> 00:28:25.530
behind on the graphics
or the devices.

00:28:25.530 --> 00:28:27.050
But it's really,
really important

00:28:27.050 --> 00:28:28.400
to focus on the user experience.

00:28:28.400 --> 00:28:31.210
People are voting with their
dollars, voting with the apps.

00:28:31.210 --> 00:28:33.664
The revolution
wasn't the iPhone.

00:28:33.664 --> 00:28:36.080
The revolution was the app
store a couple of months later.

00:28:36.080 --> 00:28:37.220
That's how we live our lives.

00:28:37.220 --> 00:28:38.511
That's where the revolution is.

00:28:41.330 --> 00:28:43.392
I know this is kind
of a weird session.

00:28:43.392 --> 00:28:44.600
It wasn't about installation.

00:28:44.600 --> 00:28:45.500
It wasn't about configuration.

00:28:45.500 --> 00:28:46.700
It wasn't about the code.

00:28:46.700 --> 00:28:48.950
It wasn't about a live
demo of the product.

00:28:48.950 --> 00:28:51.230
It was around all
those other bits

00:28:51.230 --> 00:28:53.390
that go around it, but
unfortunately are just

00:28:53.390 --> 00:28:55.580
as necessary to be successful.

00:28:55.580 --> 00:29:00.050
And I want you all to have
very successful API projects

00:29:00.050 --> 00:29:02.720
so I can tell great stories as I
fly around the world about what

00:29:02.720 --> 00:29:03.560
you guys have done.

00:29:03.560 --> 00:29:05.810
If you want to dig deeper,
we do have a few resources.

00:29:05.810 --> 00:29:08.450
We have a micro site
dedicated to this topic.

00:29:08.450 --> 00:29:12.170
And there's a brand new
hot-off-the-presses ebook about

00:29:12.170 --> 00:29:15.320
how to stop thinking about APIs
as projects and start thinking

00:29:15.320 --> 00:29:18.150
them as products.

00:29:18.150 --> 00:29:22.060
Now with that said, I'm going
to pause here for a moment

00:29:22.060 --> 00:29:25.210
and allow you guys time to put
any questions into the system

00:29:25.210 --> 00:29:26.110
you'd like to put in.

00:29:26.110 --> 00:29:27.490
And I'll be back
in just a second

00:29:27.490 --> 00:29:29.030
to answer some of
those questions.

00:29:29.030 --> 00:29:29.529
Thank you.

00:31:59.370 --> 00:32:00.076
OK, everyone.

00:32:00.076 --> 00:32:01.200
Thanks for staying with us.

00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:02.574
We did get a couple
of questions.

00:32:02.574 --> 00:32:05.370
I'd like to quickly
address those.

00:32:05.370 --> 00:32:07.377
One question was, can we
configure Google Cloud

00:32:07.377 --> 00:32:09.960
products, you know, some of the
existing Google Cloud products

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:14.190
and APIs such as BigQuery
or others using Apigee?

00:32:14.190 --> 00:32:16.860
So the answer is yes, of course.

00:32:16.860 --> 00:32:21.870
We have a lot of customers who
front many other backend APIs,

00:32:21.870 --> 00:32:23.220
so various maps.

00:32:23.220 --> 00:32:26.430
If you think about some of
the commerce applications,

00:32:26.430 --> 00:32:28.530
some of the transaction
and settlement software.

00:32:28.530 --> 00:32:32.730
A lot of those third party
APIs get fronted in Apigee.

00:32:32.730 --> 00:32:37.020
So yes, of course, you're always
able to do any API, including

00:32:37.020 --> 00:32:38.490
the Google Cloud APIs.

00:32:38.490 --> 00:32:40.890
Now, the subtext
to this might be,

00:32:40.890 --> 00:32:43.170
since Apigee is part
of Google Cloud,

00:32:43.170 --> 00:32:45.240
is there some kind of
deeper integration?

00:32:45.240 --> 00:32:47.710
And the answer is not yet.

00:32:47.710 --> 00:32:51.450
But again, Google APIs are
so well-designed that it

00:32:51.450 --> 00:32:54.490
is very easy to plug
Apigee in front of them.

00:32:54.490 --> 00:32:58.050
It really doesn't take
a lot of work at all.

00:32:58.050 --> 00:33:00.050
There's a few other
questions-- so

00:33:00.050 --> 00:33:02.190
where does Apigee fit
into the architecture

00:33:02.190 --> 00:33:03.207
that we already have?

00:33:03.207 --> 00:33:04.790
Hopefully I convinced
you a little bit

00:33:04.790 --> 00:33:07.248
that this is very different
than an integration technology.

00:33:07.248 --> 00:33:11.010
You probably have ESB,
some SOA technologies.

00:33:11.010 --> 00:33:14.490
And this would generally
sit in front of that.

00:33:14.490 --> 00:33:18.180
And so Apigee is a great
bridge from legacy technologies

00:33:18.180 --> 00:33:21.450
to the cloud technologies,
from legacy technologies

00:33:21.450 --> 00:33:25.260
to modern apps, Android
and iPhone apps and things

00:33:25.260 --> 00:33:26.675
like that.

00:33:26.675 --> 00:33:28.050
And, you know, it
would generally

00:33:28.050 --> 00:33:31.290
sit behind the firewall
on the load balancers

00:33:31.290 --> 00:33:33.120
as one of the edge technologies.

00:33:33.120 --> 00:33:34.770
That's what we
called it Apigee Edge

00:33:34.770 --> 00:33:38.290
as it's meant to sit in front of
all those backend technologies.

00:33:38.290 --> 00:33:40.740
And if you hit our website
or you ping us afterwards,

00:33:40.740 --> 00:33:42.698
we'd be happy to do an
architectural deep dive.

00:33:42.698 --> 00:33:45.030
And you can see some various
diagrams and white papers

00:33:45.030 --> 00:33:47.850
where we go into this.

00:33:47.850 --> 00:33:50.610
How do we start
focusing on consumption

00:33:50.610 --> 00:33:51.700
instead of exposure?

00:33:51.700 --> 00:33:54.360
This is dangerous, because
a lot of legacy vendors

00:33:54.360 --> 00:33:56.940
are telling you, well, we've
added some bells and whistles

00:33:56.940 --> 00:33:58.860
that allow you to do
exposure and make it

00:33:58.860 --> 00:34:02.469
sound like that's the answer.

00:34:02.469 --> 00:34:04.260
I was working at a
large telecommunications

00:34:04.260 --> 00:34:08.390
company one day, doing a seminar
on API design best practice.

00:34:08.390 --> 00:34:09.389
And they kept stumbling.

00:34:09.389 --> 00:34:12.730
They were trying to
design their texting API,

00:34:12.730 --> 00:34:14.340
write their SMS API.

00:34:14.340 --> 00:34:17.310
And they kept having like
100 data fields in there.

00:34:17.310 --> 00:34:19.920
And I kept saying to myself,
I'm not an expert in this field,

00:34:19.920 --> 00:34:23.370
but it seems like you've got
a a sender, a time stamp,

00:34:23.370 --> 00:34:25.019
content, and an
array of receivers,

00:34:25.019 --> 00:34:27.610
so like four fields,
something like that?

00:34:27.610 --> 00:34:30.330
I mean, whatever it is, it's
closer to four than it is 100.

00:34:30.330 --> 00:34:32.920
And I said to them, why
do you think it's 100?

00:34:32.920 --> 00:34:34.600
You're the experts,
but why is this?

00:34:34.600 --> 00:34:36.183
And they said, well,
you know, we just

00:34:36.183 --> 00:34:38.280
updated this system to 7.2.8.

00:34:38.280 --> 00:34:40.139
Nobody cares.

00:34:40.139 --> 00:34:44.370
Nobody should ever care or
know about that information.

00:34:44.370 --> 00:34:48.300
With APIs, you've got to
think outside, not inside.

00:34:48.300 --> 00:34:50.100
Somebody outside of
our organization,

00:34:50.100 --> 00:34:52.650
outside of our
department, whatever,

00:34:52.650 --> 00:34:54.000
what are they coming to us for?

00:34:54.000 --> 00:34:55.420
Like, why do they come here?

00:34:55.420 --> 00:34:57.350
Why do they come to us?

00:34:57.350 --> 00:35:00.240
And make that as easy
to consume as possible.

00:35:00.240 --> 00:35:05.180
Make your best goods on offer
as a super consumable API.

00:35:05.180 --> 00:35:07.740
That's the way to start
think about consumption is

00:35:07.740 --> 00:35:09.740
the other parts of the
business that come to us,

00:35:09.740 --> 00:35:10.770
why do they come to us?

00:35:10.770 --> 00:35:12.950
What's our core
offering for them?

00:35:12.950 --> 00:35:15.290
Our customers, what's the
main reason they come to us?

00:35:15.290 --> 00:35:17.960
How can I make that as easy
to consume as possible?

00:35:17.960 --> 00:35:21.650
Instead of, what are the
advantages or limitations

00:35:21.650 --> 00:35:24.210
of my backend systems or what
kind of version or vendor

00:35:24.210 --> 00:35:26.120
I've got in my backend stuff?

00:35:26.120 --> 00:35:28.130
And one question I get
all the time is, Brian,

00:35:28.130 --> 00:35:30.950
you keep telling us not to
think of the APIs as a project.

00:35:30.950 --> 00:35:32.900
Think of them as a product.

00:35:32.900 --> 00:35:34.940
But we're starting
with a project.

00:35:34.940 --> 00:35:36.110
Are we wrong?

00:35:36.110 --> 00:35:37.640
Is this the wrong thing to do?

00:35:37.640 --> 00:35:38.580
And of course not.

00:35:38.580 --> 00:35:41.150
You often, in a
big organization,

00:35:41.150 --> 00:35:44.990
will have a project that's
funded, that's staffed,

00:35:44.990 --> 00:35:48.440
that has a charter,
that's on your portfolio.

00:35:48.440 --> 00:35:50.330
That's great.

00:35:50.330 --> 00:35:53.360
That's a perfect opportunity
to introduce these technologies

00:35:53.360 --> 00:35:55.160
and introduce this
methodology, as long

00:35:55.160 --> 00:35:58.360
as you're aware that this
goes well beyond that project.

00:35:58.360 --> 00:36:01.700
APIs should be the center
of every project you do.

00:36:01.700 --> 00:36:05.000
Your API platform should
span lines of business

00:36:05.000 --> 00:36:06.860
and be across the entire brand.

00:36:06.860 --> 00:36:08.720
As long as you go into
that single project

00:36:08.720 --> 00:36:11.027
with that mindset,
you won't make those--

00:36:11.027 --> 00:36:12.860
you know, I'm a lifelong
software architect.

00:36:12.860 --> 00:36:15.470
Those 1,000 decisions that
you make, none of which

00:36:15.470 --> 00:36:17.930
by themselves are critical,
but if you make them

00:36:17.930 --> 00:36:19.700
all in the wrong
direction, you're

00:36:19.700 --> 00:36:22.596
going to end up in a
very different outcome.

00:36:22.596 --> 00:36:24.970
And so if you think of this
as not just for this project,

00:36:24.970 --> 00:36:26.810
that this is the
future of the company,

00:36:26.810 --> 00:36:29.790
then the project is a
great way to get started.

00:36:33.640 --> 00:36:36.440
And to me, after these
kinds of sessions,

00:36:36.440 --> 00:36:39.380
some people say, well, Brian,
I know you love linguistics,

00:36:39.380 --> 00:36:40.880
you love language,
things like that.

00:36:40.880 --> 00:36:43.820
Is this just fundamentally
a semantic issue?

00:36:43.820 --> 00:36:45.382
And I truly don't believe that.

00:36:45.382 --> 00:36:46.840
Any of you who have
built software,

00:36:46.840 --> 00:36:48.530
which I assume is
most of you, know

00:36:48.530 --> 00:36:50.420
that it's not just what
color the button is

00:36:50.420 --> 00:36:51.753
and where it goes on the screen.

00:36:51.753 --> 00:36:54.470
There are 1,000 questions
and design decisions

00:36:54.470 --> 00:36:59.030
that we have to do along
the creation of a product.

00:36:59.030 --> 00:37:02.270
And if we make all
those decisions

00:37:02.270 --> 00:37:05.480
in a way that thinks
of this in a very

00:37:05.480 --> 00:37:08.926
inside first traditional
20th century kind of mindset,

00:37:08.926 --> 00:37:11.300
the result is going to be,
unfortunately, what we see all

00:37:11.300 --> 00:37:14.120
over the app stores right now,
which is traditional companies

00:37:14.120 --> 00:37:17.690
just kind of looking silly with
their apps, how far behind they

00:37:17.690 --> 00:37:19.049
are graphically and usability.

00:37:19.049 --> 00:37:20.090
And we're all frustrated.

00:37:20.090 --> 00:37:22.209
We all have companies
that we're customers of

00:37:22.209 --> 00:37:23.750
and we're frustrated
with their apps.

00:37:23.750 --> 00:37:27.290
And why can't this be more like
some Silicon Valley startup's

00:37:27.290 --> 00:37:29.240
fantastic, glamorous app?

00:37:29.240 --> 00:37:30.530
And I think that's the harm.

00:37:30.530 --> 00:37:32.060
I think the harm
is thinking that

00:37:32.060 --> 00:37:34.070
what got you there
in the 20th century

00:37:34.070 --> 00:37:36.740
is what's going to win in the
21st century, when empirically

00:37:36.740 --> 00:37:38.870
we know that that's not true.

00:37:38.870 --> 00:37:43.010
And so not focusing on the
experience, the customer

00:37:43.010 --> 00:37:45.500
experience, not
focusing on speed,

00:37:45.500 --> 00:37:48.050
not focusing on working
across organizational trust

00:37:48.050 --> 00:37:50.690
boundaries, I don't think
that's semantic at all.

00:37:50.690 --> 00:37:55.130
I think that fundamentally, like
statistically, will lead you

00:37:55.130 --> 00:37:58.520
to situations that are conducive
to failing in the 21st century,

00:37:58.520 --> 00:38:01.130
just as if you're API
centric, and you're

00:38:01.130 --> 00:38:04.460
thinking of things in terms
of products and consumability

00:38:04.460 --> 00:38:05.560
and customer experience.

00:38:05.560 --> 00:38:08.060
It doesn't guarantee
success, but it

00:38:08.060 --> 00:38:11.510
creates conditions that
are conducive to success

00:38:11.510 --> 00:38:14.810
in the current digital
app-based, 21st century

00:38:14.810 --> 00:38:17.390
marketplace that we
all have to compete in,

00:38:17.390 --> 00:38:20.060
whether we like it or not,
in every region of the globe,

00:38:20.060 --> 00:38:21.300
in every vertical.

00:38:21.300 --> 00:38:22.970
We're all doing
business in 2018,

00:38:22.970 --> 00:38:25.850
and this is simply what our
employees, our developers,

00:38:25.850 --> 00:38:31.260
our partners, and our customers
have come to expect from us.

00:38:31.260 --> 00:38:33.261
So I think that's all the
questions that we had.

00:38:33.261 --> 00:38:35.718
And I know sometimes it's hard
when you're on the spot here

00:38:35.718 --> 00:38:37.100
to think of a
question right now.

00:38:37.100 --> 00:38:38.750
If you think one, when you're
taking a shower tomorrow

00:38:38.750 --> 00:38:41.390
morning and, oh, I wish I'd
asked Brian this question,

00:38:41.390 --> 00:38:42.890
feel free to ping us any time.

00:38:42.890 --> 00:38:43.564
Reach out to us.

00:38:43.564 --> 00:38:44.480
We'd be happy to talk.

00:38:44.480 --> 00:38:46.180
We love talking
about this stuff.

00:38:46.180 --> 00:38:48.800
We get very fired up
talking about this stuff.

00:38:48.800 --> 00:38:50.540
And otherwise, we
hope that you'll

00:38:50.540 --> 00:38:55.790
tune in next week, next Tuesday,
for the next Cloud OnAir

00:38:55.790 --> 00:38:57.000
webinar.

00:38:57.000 --> 00:38:58.470
My name is Brian Pagano.

00:38:58.470 --> 00:39:00.110
I come to you from Google Cloud.

00:39:00.110 --> 00:39:02.800
Thank you so much
